  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Introduced in the Acquisitions Incorporated sourcebook, the Verdan is a race\n\tdescended from goblinoids native to the Forgotten Realms. They resemble humans\n\twith backswept, pointed ears and skin in shades of green. They lack a distinct\n\tculture, but they are described as empathetic and opposed to tyranny, but\n\ttolerant of rules and laws."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the Verdan screams “make a Charisma-based spellcaster”. Constitution and Charisma increases are obvious, and Black-Blood Healing is more effective if you have a smaller hit die. Limited Telepathy and Persuasion allow you to communicate easily and serve as your party’s Face, and while Telepathic Insight isn’t as good as Gnome Cunning or Vedalken Dispassion, it’s still pretty good since Intelligence saves are so incredibly rare."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the custom origin rules in place, the Verdan overlaps with other races\n\tsignificantly. They fall somewhere between the Kalasthar and the Vedalken,\n\tgetting Advantage on two mental saves and proficiency in one skill. Their\n\ttelepathy only allots communicating simple concepts, so it’s not quite as good\n\tas the Kalashtar’s. Beyond that, the Verdan’s signature trait is Black Blood\n\tHealing, which provides some insurance against poor hit die rolls. Like both\n\tthe Kalashtar and the Vedalken, these traits work well for basically any\n\tclass, but don’t predisipose you to any specific capability, so the variance\n\tin class options is typically small."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
